<section class="article-header"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/">International Clients</a> / Land Registry Appointment</div><div class="tag">Real Estate Law · Title Transfer</div><h1>Turkish Land Registry Appointment for Foreign Buyers: What to Expect</h1><p class="article-summary">The title-transfer appointment is the visible end of a property purchase, but most problems should be solved before that day. Foreign buyers benefit from knowing which documents, interpreter arrangements, payments and title details must already be in order.</p><div class="article-meta">Published: 8 September 2026 · Ozan Ferhat Şen, Attorney at Law</div></div></section>
<section class="wrap article-layout"><article class="article-body">
<p>For a foreign buyer, the Turkish land registry appointment can feel like the decisive moment of the purchase. In reality, it should be the final execution step of a transaction that has already been legally checked.</p>
<h2>1. The property details should be settled before the appointment</h2>
<p>The buyer should already know the registered owner, parcel or independent-unit details and whether the title carries mortgages, attachments, annotations or third-party rights. The appointment is not the ideal moment to discover that the property record differs from the sales presentation.</p>
<h2>2. Identity documents must be consistent</h2>
<p>Passport information, translations and other identity records should match. Differences in spelling or transliteration can create avoidable complications, especially where documents have been prepared in several countries or languages.</p>
<h2>3. Interpreter requirements should be arranged in advance</h2>
<p>Where the buyer cannot conduct the official procedure in Turkish, an interpreter may be required. The practical requirement should be confirmed in advance so that the transfer is not delayed by a missing interpreter or unsuitable document.</p>
<h2>4. Payment timing deserves its own plan</h2>
<p>Buyer and seller should understand when the purchase price will be paid and how that timing relates to the transfer. A property transaction should not rely on vague assumptions about whether money is transferred before, during or after registration.</p>
<h2>5. A power of attorney changes who attends, not what must be checked</h2>
<p>If a representative attends for the buyer, the power of attorney must contain the authority required for the specific transfer. The title, contract and payment structure still require the same legal attention as a transaction completed personally.</p>
<h2>6. Fees, taxes and transaction costs should not be a surprise</h2>
<p>Before the appointment, the parties should know which official fees and other costs apply and which party is expected to bear them under the transaction documents.</p>
<h2>7. What should already be checked?</h2>
<ul><li>Registered owner and property identifiers</li><li>Mortgages, attachments and annotations</li><li>Buyer’s eligibility to acquire the property</li><li>Required identity documents and translations</li><li>Interpreter arrangements where applicable</li><li>Power of attorney if a representative will attend</li><li>Payment sequence</li><li>Official fees and transaction costs</li><li>Any contract conditions that must be satisfied before transfer</li></ul>
<h2>The appointment should be boring</h2>
<p>That is usually a good sign. The safest land-registry appointment is one where the difficult questions have already been answered and the parties are simply completing a transaction whose legal structure is understood.</p>
